WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.813 --> 00:00:17.350
SG: Hi, my name is Sarah Goodwin and today we are talking to Susan McConnell

00:00:17.351 --> 00:00:20.953
who is the Susan B. Ford Professor at Stanford University

00:00:20.954 --> 00:00:26.759
and studies developmental neurobiology, and she is also an avid nature and wildlife photographer.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.061
So, thank you for joining us today.

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:31.063
SM: It's my pleasure. Sarah, thank you.

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:35.735
SG: And I'd thought I'd start off by just asking you a little bit about your scientific background,

00:00:35.736 --> 00:00:40.573
and how you got interested in science and what led you to pursue a career in basic research.

00:00:40.574 --> 00:00:44.010
SM: So, I'm a developmental neurobiologist at Stanford.

00:00:44.011 --> 00:00:50.416
I've had a lab there for 22 years. I was a graduate student at Harvard and a post-doc at Stanford.

00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:55.188
But, I think my journey into developmental neurobiology is really intrinsically connected

00:00:55.189 --> 00:00:57.790
with my interest in wildlife photography.

00:00:57.791 --> 00:01:04.997
So when I was a little kid I used to go out and watch animals as much as I could.

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:10.703
And that included sitting for hours at a wood pile where there were big families of woodrats,

00:01:10.704 --> 00:01:14.974
and I would watch them, and I'd be fascinated by the social lives of these rodents.

00:01:14.975 --> 00:01:22.014
So when I was maybe 10 or 11 years old, I thought, "Oh, you know, I love horses. I love to ride."

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:23.349
I thought I'd be a horse trainer.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:27.820
But then I saw a National Geographic special on TV that featured Jane Goodall

00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:30.957
and the chimpanzees of Gombe in Tanzania.

00:01:30.958 --> 00:01:39.832
And there was this extraordinary view of... romantic view of this beautiful young woman

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:45.438
who is spending her life in nature, studying our closest relatives,

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:49.342
getting close to these animals, getting them to trust her,

00:01:49.343 --> 00:01:54.680
and seeing amazing things about their behavior that now we know inform us about

00:01:54.681 --> 00:02:00.419
our own origins and our similarities and differences to our closest genetic cousins.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:05.024
So, I was so inspired by seeing Jane Goodall that I wanted to be a scientist.

00:02:05.025 --> 00:02:11.864
That led me into initial forays into looking into animal behavior mostly in the laboratory,

00:02:11.865 --> 00:02:18.905
and that then led to an interest in the difference in behaviors that are innate versus learned.

00:02:18.906 --> 00:02:24.610
So, how is it that some animals are just born with the ability to exhibit very complex behaviors

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:29.215
and other behaviors have to be learned steadily and progressively, being modeled?

00:02:29.216 --> 00:02:34.220
So, I thought about behavior and innate versus learned.

00:02:34.221 --> 00:02:36.489
What is that really talking about? It's talking about the brain.

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:39.192
It's talking about neural circuits and how they get wired in.

00:02:39.193 --> 00:02:43.963
What kinds of circuitry are hardwired to enable the performance of these complex behaviors

00:02:43.964 --> 00:02:49.702
and how do circuits get modified? And then that ultimately led me to my interest now

00:02:49.703 --> 00:02:54.340
as a developmental neurobiologist in how those circuits form during development.

00:02:54.341 --> 00:02:59.712
So, it's a long, but actually pretty linear progression

00:02:59.713 --> 00:03:04.116
from being fascinated by animal behavior to studying brain development in the lab.

00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:06.285
SG: So, you've always had an interest in animal behavior.

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:10.923
What led you into the photography aspect of observing animal behavior?

00:03:10.924 --> 00:03:16.162
SM: There is a way of being present when you're taking pictures that is

00:03:16.163 --> 00:03:20.166
extraordinarily thoughtful and engaged

00:03:20.167 --> 00:03:26.873
because as a photographer you're not just responding to what you see, you're really searching for

00:03:26.874 --> 00:03:35.381
how to communicate with an audience an idea, an emotion, a sense of what you're seeing.

00:03:35.382 --> 00:03:42.355
Photography is a form of visual communication and searching for that message is interesting.

00:03:42.356 --> 00:03:46.659
For example, I was in the Makgadikgadi Pan of Botswana

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.595
and had gone there specifically to photograph meerkats,

00:03:49.596 --> 00:03:54.834
these wonderful desert creatures that are extraordinarily social.

00:03:54.835 --> 00:03:58.137
And I went there because there was a very well known group of meerkats that

00:03:58.138 --> 00:04:00.573
were highly habituated to people and as a photographer,

00:04:00.574 --> 00:04:04.110
photographing animals that are habituated, which is different than trained,

00:04:04.111 --> 00:04:09.649
but habituated animals really let's you get up close and personal with them.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:16.656
When I got to this site, what I first found was that new babies had just emerged from the den.

00:04:16.657 --> 00:04:21.861
So my first pictures were really focusing on the wonder of these new babies.

00:04:21.862 --> 00:04:25.932
And as the babies actually started to interact with their parents,

00:04:25.933 --> 00:04:30.770
I started to play around with light, back lighting, front lighting,

00:04:30.771 --> 00:04:34.273
the grouping of the animals, the behavior and so on.

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:37.009
And so you don't want to take the same picture over and over.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:40.813
You want to change your point of view and try to find a unique perspective.

00:04:40.814 --> 00:04:47.920
What happened then was really cool. A huge Marabou Stork, which is a predator of small animals like meerkats,

00:04:47.921 --> 00:04:51.424
landed and this whole group of meerkats that had been dispersed

00:04:51.425 --> 00:04:54.594
and feeding, gathered together for safety.

00:04:54.595 --> 00:05:01.634
And then I started to play with a set of images that explored the geometry of that gathering

00:05:01.635 --> 00:05:04.737
as they were all focused on a particular point in space.

00:05:04.738 --> 00:05:08.241
But I was still searching for what you can think of as an image that would really

00:05:08.242 --> 00:05:11.043
convey something about not only these animals and their behavior,

00:05:11.044 --> 00:05:15.381
but how it is that they survive in this environment.

00:05:15.382 --> 00:05:19.552
And it was at that point that I thought, "Oh my god, I've got the wrong lens on my camera.

00:05:19.553 --> 00:05:24.657
I've been shooting them close, close, close and exploring their relationships and their geometry,

00:05:24.658 --> 00:05:29.095
but they're exposed. They're exposed next to a predator."

00:05:29.096 --> 00:05:32.298
So I changed to a wide-angle lens. Got in front of the animals.

00:05:32.299 --> 00:05:35.034
The sun was setting. The light was beautiful.

00:05:35.035 --> 00:05:40.106
I had to put a flash on just to light them a little bit from the front to fill up their faces.

00:05:40.107 --> 00:05:45.678
And that then resulted in the image from that couple of hours with meerkats that

00:05:45.679 --> 00:05:51.817
I think of as an image that communicates not only about these animals,

00:05:51.818 --> 00:05:56.455
but their relationships and their vulnerability in the place where they live.

00:05:56.456 --> 00:06:00.860
SG: Do you think your approach to photography can almost be described as scientific?

00:06:00.861 --> 00:06:05.598
SM: Part of the art of photography is responding to light,

00:06:05.599 --> 00:06:08.801
and if you're a wildlife photographer, to behavior

00:06:08.802 --> 00:06:15.541
and conceptualizing how it is to represent that moment through the camera.

00:06:15.542 --> 00:06:21.480
But photography is also highly technical art because the camera is a machine.

00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:28.688
So in order to understand how to translate what's happening out there

00:06:28.689 --> 00:06:33.025
into the vision I have of how to communicate that, that I have in my mind.

00:06:33.026 --> 00:06:41.334
I need to understand enough basic optics and physics to put the right settings on the camera,

00:06:41.335 --> 00:06:47.006
to hit the right depth of field, to know whether I want a sharp image or a blurred image.

00:06:47.007 --> 00:06:51.344
What's going to communicate the feeling that I want to transmit through this image.

00:06:51.345 --> 00:06:57.350
And also, there are properties of the sensor and how it detects light

00:06:57.351 --> 00:07:07.193
that determine if one will even try to take a picture under particular lighting or circumstances.

00:07:07.194 --> 00:07:11.464
So, photography is one of those fields where

00:07:11.465 --> 00:07:15.134
the intersection between art and science are very, very close.

00:07:15.135 --> 00:07:20.039
SG: So can you tell us a little bit about one of your most memorable experiences in the field?

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:24.210
SM: I think for me, one of the most satisfying experiences and exciting experiences

00:07:24.211 --> 00:07:30.316
that I've had was nearly three weeks spent at a single water hole, one water hole in Namibia

00:07:30.317 --> 00:07:36.355
with Caitlin O'Connell-Rodwell who is a researcher who's been studying elephants for 25 years.

00:07:36.356 --> 00:07:41.260
I was given the assignment to shoot pictures of the story that Caitlin was writing

00:07:41.261 --> 00:07:44.430
about the social relationships between bull elephants.

00:07:44.431 --> 00:07:50.369
And largely our sense of male elephants is that they're mostly solitary.

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:52.839
And they might hang out with buddies at watering holes for a little while,

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:57.243
but Caitlin had discovered that these male elephants formed

00:07:57.244 --> 00:08:02.348
very long term, hierarchical relationships that involve coalitions,

00:08:02.349 --> 00:08:07.854
and you know, like a boss and underlings who

00:08:07.855 --> 00:08:11.991
sort of carry out the boss's orders and it's very interesting and fascinating.

00:08:11.992 --> 00:08:17.463
By spending that period of time with Caitlin and her colleagues,

00:08:17.464 --> 00:08:21.033
I learned an enormous amount about elephant behavior.

00:08:21.034 --> 00:08:24.871
I was able to see things in the way that they interact with one another

00:08:24.872 --> 00:08:29.609
the way that they approach one another, sometimes with anxiety and sometimes with aggression.

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:34.213
They can square off and have really interesting fights with one another.

00:08:34.214 --> 00:08:39.418
There's also a very specific behavior that Caitlin and her colleagues had observed again and again

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:45.925
where a subservient elephant puts his trunk to the mouth of a more dominant bull.

00:08:45.926 --> 00:08:50.696
It's an appeasement gesture. It's the same thing that a baby elephant would do to his mother.

00:08:50.697 --> 00:08:54.634
And I also got to observe not only the relationship between the bulls,

00:08:54.635 --> 00:08:59.639
but the arrivals of these large breeding herds of elephants as they come to the water hole.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.007
There's chaos! There's dust flying!

00:09:01.008 --> 00:09:07.146
And especially if I was in an underground bunker, very, very close to some of these elephants,

00:09:07.147 --> 00:09:13.853
they would get scent of me and then give these extraordinarily aggressive displays

00:09:13.854 --> 00:09:16.656
aimed at me. Thank goodness I was safe.

00:09:16.657 --> 00:09:21.794
So that was a privilege, to be able to get so close to elephants

00:09:21.795 --> 00:09:27.133
to see so much of their behavior that you wouldn't... I wouldn't have observed

00:09:27.134 --> 00:09:30.303
on my own without being around researchers.

00:09:30.304 --> 00:09:33.973
It gave me the ability to predict behavior accurately, too.

00:09:33.974 --> 00:09:37.610
I knew when I could just sit back and relax and watch the scene,

00:09:37.611 --> 00:09:43.516
and when I needed to grab both cameras and be ready because all chaos was going to break loose.

00:09:43.517 --> 00:09:47.286
SG: Could you tell us a little bit more about some of the places you've been to?

00:09:47.287 --> 00:09:51.624
SM: There are just so many extraordinary places on the planet, including in our own backyard

00:09:51.625 --> 00:09:57.463
that have amazing animals, behavior, experiences one can have with nature.

00:09:57.464 --> 00:10:02.335
I'm addicted to Africa. I'm going on my 15th trip there next month,

00:10:02.336 --> 00:10:06.572
which sounds like a lot, but it's nothing compared to the people who actually live there.

00:10:06.573 --> 00:10:12.545
I had a really remarkable opportunity to spend some time with mountain gorillas

00:10:12.546 --> 00:10:17.016
first in Uganda several years ago and then more recently in Rwanda.

00:10:17.017 --> 00:10:21.287
As extraordinary as it is to watch animals

00:10:21.288 --> 00:10:31.130
living, playing, fighting, nursing their babies, eating. All of those things are amazing.

00:10:31.131 --> 00:10:37.303
But for me the most emotional moment that I had in Rwanda was

00:10:37.304 --> 00:10:42.241
at a moment when a group of gorillas were very close to a stone fence

00:10:42.242 --> 00:10:45.311
that separated the Volcanoes National Park,

00:10:45.312 --> 00:10:49.749
a very small reserve where some of the last remaining mountain gorillas are protected,

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:53.886
from neighboring areas, villages, that are cultivated farm land.

00:10:53.887 --> 00:11:00.126
And one by one, these gorillas went and sat on this stone fence looking out.

00:11:00.127 --> 00:11:06.599
And in the distance were some people from the local village looking back at the gorillas.

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:12.738
To me, that sort of recognition by these animals that this was a boundary,

00:11:12.739 --> 00:11:17.944
and the way that they looked out at land that used to be theirs

00:11:17.945 --> 00:11:24.650
and yet is preserving them now, the local people and we as tourists, preserving them now,

00:11:24.651 --> 00:11:30.990
was an extraordinarily moving experience. And I think a picture like this one

00:11:30.991 --> 00:11:37.063
really captures that complex relationship that we have with the natural world right now.

00:11:37.064 --> 00:11:42.435
SG: Have your experiences as a nature photographer affected your life as a scientist?

00:11:42.436 --> 00:11:48.508
SM: My experiences as a photographer have had a lot of effects on my life as a scientist.

00:11:48.509 --> 00:11:52.678
I think most importantly it's made me feel happy and whole.

00:11:52.679 --> 00:11:56.682
That there is something I feel passionate about.

00:11:56.683 --> 00:12:02.688
When things in the lab aren't going well that I can explore, even in my own backyard,

00:12:02.689 --> 00:12:11.964
the elephant seals at Año Nuevo or the hummingbirds that frequent our literal backyards,

00:12:11.965 --> 00:12:17.303
or the burrowing owls that live around here, these teeny little owls that are just adorable.

00:12:17.304 --> 00:12:22.975
So it's very easy actually to go quite close to home and to have

00:12:22.976 --> 00:12:26.913
one of these experiences that makes you feel rejuvenated and refreshed.

00:12:26.914 --> 00:12:30.483
SG: So as you became more passionate about nature and wildlife photography,

00:12:30.484 --> 00:12:33.452
how did you balance that with your career as a scientist?

00:12:33.453 --> 00:12:38.224
SM: Yeah, there are a lot of different passions that I think we scientists have.

00:12:38.225 --> 00:12:42.528
Photography being my example right now that can take a lot of time.

00:12:42.529 --> 00:12:48.267
And I think for me it was very important to realize that there are seasons in a career.

00:12:48.268 --> 00:12:54.574
There are times of one's career, for example the time at which you're first starting your lab,

00:12:54.575 --> 00:13:00.379
where you need to immerse yourself 24/7

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:04.584
in building your group, in getting your research off the ground

00:13:04.585 --> 00:13:11.924
and so these are not times when you'd want to take off for a month and photograph gorillas in Rwanda

00:13:11.925 --> 00:13:15.862
because you're needed at home, and your career depends on

00:13:15.863 --> 00:13:21.234
that sort of real, deep investment of self in your science.

00:13:21.235 --> 00:13:28.107
And yet, as a lab matures, and as students become more self reliant, and

00:13:28.108 --> 00:13:36.182
the lab reaches a point where it can function for chunks of time without, for example me being there.

00:13:36.183 --> 00:13:42.788
So it's always a juggling act but I think that once priorities shift, not only over a career,

00:13:42.789 --> 00:13:45.691
but even from month to month and from year to year,

00:13:45.692 --> 00:13:50.229
a time at which one's students need you because they've got to get their papers out

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:52.198
is not a time to abandon ship.

00:13:52.199 --> 00:13:55.067
And then there are other times during the year that are more flexible,

00:13:55.068 --> 00:13:59.273
and it's easier to pay some attention at least to these other parts of life.

